1
00:00:02,950 --> 00:00:04,343
Now flip it around.

2
00:00:04,343 --> 00:00:05,574
What if the person on the other side

3
00:00:05,574 --> 00:00:07,713
reads the rules better than the company?

4
00:00:07,963 --> 00:00:11,957
In 1999, Healthy Choice offered airline miles

5
00:00:11,957 --> 00:00:14,430
for barcodes from its products:

6
00:00:14,430 --> 00:00:16,879
500 miles for 10 barcodes,

7
00:00:16,879 --> 00:00:19,155
doubled if you mailed them early.

8
00:00:19,155 --> 00:00:21,187
Engineer David Phillips looked for

9
00:00:21,187 --> 00:00:23,857
the cheapest barcode in the store.

10
00:00:23,857 --> 00:00:25,552
Chocolate pudding.

11
00:00:25,552 --> 00:00:27,059
25 cents a cup.

12
00:00:27,309 --> 00:00:29,457
He bought over 12,000 cups,

13
00:00:29,457 --> 00:00:33,114
had Salvation Army volunteers peel the barcodes,

14
00:00:33,114 --> 00:00:35,900
and gave the pudding to people in need.

15
00:00:35,900 --> 00:00:40,324
About $3,000 turned into over a million miles.

16
00:00:40,324 --> 00:00:42,983
The donation even got him a tax write-off,

17
00:00:42,983 --> 00:00:45,073
and Hollywood later borrowed his pudding plan

18
00:00:45,073 --> 00:00:46,981
for an Adam Sandler movie.

19
00:00:47,231 --> 00:00:48,601
He didn't cheat.

20
00:00:48,601 --> 00:00:51,016
He did exactly what the rules paid for.

21
00:00:51,016 --> 00:00:53,442
The company wanted people to buy its food.

22
00:00:53,442 --> 00:00:55,126
It paid for barcodes.

23
00:00:55,126 --> 00:00:57,680
And a barcode is just a barcode.

24
00:00:57,680 --> 00:00:59,421
Phillips later put it simply:

25
00:00:59,421 --> 00:01:00,989
"Companies still make mistakes

26
00:01:00,989 --> 00:01:02,738
like this all the time."
